---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import analytics from '../../assets/dvzz-analytics.png';

// Orders per month from the store's sales export (aggregated, no customer data).
const months = [
  ['Jun 2021', 48], ['Jul', 20], ['Aug', 70], ['Sep', 5], ['Oct', 2], ['Nov', 11], ['Dec', 0],
  ['Jan 2022', 0], ['Feb', 25], ['Mar', 1], ['Apr', 6], ['May', 3], ['Jun', 0], ['Jul', 4],
  ['Aug', 2], ['Sep', 1], ['Oct', 0], ['Nov', 1], ['Dec', 0],
] as [string, number][];
const max = Math.max(...months.map(([, n]) => n));
---

<Base
  title="DvzzPins · David Salazar"
  description="I ran DvzzPins, a Shopify pin store, from product development and supplier proofs to launches and customer messages. Here are the store's numbers and what they showed."
  current="work"
>
  <div class="case">
    <header class="case-head">
      <div class="col">
        <h1 data-reveal>DvzzPins</h1>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>
          A Shopify store I ran on my own from December 2020 to October 2022, from product idea to
          supplier proof to launch.
        </p>
        <dl class="spec" data-reveal>
          <dt>What</dt>
          <dd>An online store selling enamel pins.</dd>
          <dt>Role</dt>
          <dd>Founder and only operator: product development, designers, suppliers, inventory, launches and customer messages.</dd>
          <dt>Tools</dt>
          <dd>Shopify, including its analytics and order exports.</dd>
          <dt>Numbers</dt>
          <dd>199 orders and $3,760 in net sales from January 2021 to December 2022.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>What I did</h2>
        <div class="runin">
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>Product development.</strong> I turned concepts into products with designers
            and manufacturers. Each design went through a supplier proof that fixed the size,
            quantity, enamel and plating type and color codes, and that carried a warning: any
            change after the printing plates are made means a new setup or mold charge. That made
            proof review the step where mistakes were cheapest to catch.
          </p>
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>Suppliers.</strong> I worked through specs and proofs with supplier GS-JJ,
            negotiated a $50 credit on a repeat mold charge, and got quotes for a charm line.
          </p>
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>Running the store.</strong> I managed inventory, planned launches and answered
            customers. I used the store's data to see what sold and when.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>The numbers</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          This is the Shopify analytics view for January 2021 to December 2022. The store's first
          orders came in June 2021.
        </p>
        <figure class="wide-shot" data-reveal style="margin:1.5rem 0">
          <Image
            src={analytics}
            alt="Shopify analytics for 2021 to 2022: total sales of $4,481, 199 orders and 4,959 store sessions, each with two large peaks in mid 2021 and one smaller peak in early 2022"
            widths={[640, 1000, 1400]}
            sizes="(min-width: 40rem) 35rem, 92vw"
          />
          <figcaption class="figure-note">
            Total sales include shipping, which is why they are higher than net sales.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <ul data-reveal>
          <li>199 orders, $3,760 in net sales and $4,481 in total sales including shipping.</li>
          <li>4,959 store sessions and an average order value of $18.32.</li>
          <li>86.6% product sell-through, with several designs selling out completely.</li>
          <li>14.8% of customers came back to order again.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>What the data showed</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          Orders came in bursts around launches. The first three months, June to August 2021,
          brought 138 of the 199 orders, and a second drop in February 2022 brought 25 more. Between
          launches, orders fell off quickly.
        </p>
        <div data-reveal>
          <div class="columns-chart" role="img" aria-label="Orders per month from June 2021 to December 2022: 48, 20, 70, 5, 2, 11, 0, 0, 25, 1, 6, 3, 0, 4, 2, 1, 0, 1, 0">
            {months.map(([label, n]) => (
              <span style={`height:${(n / max) * 100}%`} title={`${label}: ${n} orders`}></span>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="columns-labels" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>Jun 2021</span><span>Feb 2022</span><span>Dec 2022</span>
          </div>
          <p class="figure-note">Orders per month, from the store's sales export.</p>
        </div>
        <p data-reveal>
          For a store like this, that means the outcome depends on how well each launch is planned
          and on whether first-time buyers come back. About one in seven did.
        </p>
        <div class="actions" data-reveal>
          <a class="btn" href="/#work">Back to work</a>
          <a class="textlink" href="/#contact">Get in touch</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
